## 1. The mandate and how we read it

We start with {{ M.W0 | musd(0) }} in cash and trade daily between a diversified equity portfolio and cash
(weight φ ∈ [0, 1]; no leverage, no shorting; costs negligible). The horizon T runs from {{ M.T_min_years | f(0) }} to
{{ M.T_max_years | f(0) }} years and is known at the start. The objective is the certainty equivalent
CE = (E[W_T^{1−γ}])^{1/(1−γ)} with γ = {{ M.gamma | f(0) }}, subject to P(W_T < {{ M.K | musd(0) }}) ≤
{{ M.breach_target | pct(0) }} from every starting condition. Our interpretations:

- **The probability is model-based, and the model is uncertain.** The constraint must hold under our estimated model
  **and** under each of four robust alternatives (§3, §6). We call the maximum over these five the *worst-case breach*.
  Stress scenarios outside that set are reported separately, including where they fail.
- **A starting condition is (σ_0, r_0).** σ_0 is the model's one-day-ahead conditional volatility and r_0 the short
  rate, both observable at t = 0. Under our model these two numbers are the complete state (§3.3). The committee can
  switch on at any (σ_0, r_0). We report five named states (the 10th, 50th, 90th and 99th percentiles of the asset's own
  σ history, plus its historical maximum) and a dense grid.
- **CE is reported for the same starting conditions**, as the excess over the cash return: CE − cash (%/yr,
  annualised). This is the strategy's annualised CE minus that of holding cash on the same simulated rate paths.

## 2. Data

**Sources.** Kenneth French Data Library: daily and monthly Fama–French factors (market = Mkt-RF + RF) and the
value-weighted 10-industry daily portfolios, {{ N.data.first }} to {{ N.data.last }} ({{ N.data.n_days | i }}
trading days). The SHA-256 of each file is in `data/raw/MANIFEST.json` and Appendix A. Nothing else is used.

**What the checks found** (`scripts/01_load_check.py` → `reports/data_check/data_check.json`):

- **Risk-free precision.** Daily RF is published to {{ N.data.rf_resolution | pct(2) }} per day (about {{ (N.data.rf_resolution * 252) | pct(1) }}/yr
  resolution) and is constant within each month. It is zero on {{ N.data.rf_zero_days | i }} days, and in
  {{ N.data.rf_months_all_zero_daily }} months every daily value is zero while the monthly rate is positive. We
  therefore fit the rate model to the **monthly** series and use daily RF only for cash accounting. Compounded daily
  vs monthly RF never differs by more than the rounding bound ({{ N.data.rf_n_flagged }} months flagged).
- **Negative rates.** The monthly RF is negative in {{ N.data.rf_n_negative_months }} months
  ({{ N.data.rf_negative_months_first }} to {{ N.data.rf_negative_months_last }}). The rate model's 1954 start excludes
  them, and it does not floor rates at zero.
- **Market return construction.** Daily (Mkt-RF + RF) compounded to months matches the monthly file with a mean
  absolute difference of {{ N.data.xcheck_mean | pct(3) }} (max {{ N.data.xcheck_max | pct(2) }},
  {{ N.data.xcheck_max_month }}).
- **Closure gaps.** {{ N.data.n_gaps }} gaps exceed {{ S.gap_report_days }} calendar days (the longest, {{ N.data.gap_max_days }} days, is the
  1933 bank holiday). Returns after a gap are scaled by √(business days spanned) for fitting only.
- **Saturday trading** until {{ N.data.sat_last_year }} (up to {{ N.data.max_saturdays }} Saturdays a year) and
  **1968** ({{ N.data.days_1968 }} days: the NYSE closed on Wednesdays during the paperwork crisis) explain every
  out-of-range year. The model runs in trading-day time.
- **Tails.** The largest daily move is {{ N.data.extreme_r | pct(1) }} ({{ N.data.extreme_day }}). Excess kurtosis of
  daily log excess returns is {{ N.data.full_exkurt | f(1) }}. The worst 252-day return is
  {{ N.data.worst_252 | pct(1) }} (ending {{ N.data.worst_252_end }}). Over the full sample,
  {{ N.data.share_252_below_5 | pct(1) }} of 252-day windows lose more than 5%.

