## 3. Model

### 3.1 Equity: GJR-GARCH(1,1) with Student-t innovations

The modelling variable is the daily log excess return x = log(1+r) − log(1+rf). Its conditional variance follows
GJR-GARCH(1,1) with leverage, and its innovations are standardised t. The conditional arithmetic premium μ_e is constant.
We compared four models on us_mkt:

{{ T.model_comparison_us_mkt }}

EGARCH-t has the best BIC, but its simulated 1–2-year tails are almost identical to GJR-t's, so the floor would not
change. GJR-t has a closed-form persistence and unconditional variance, and its σ_0 is a transparent state. GARCH-t is
kept as an alternative data-generating process in the robust set. Its slow vol mean reversion produces the fat
multi-year tails that GJR misses. Fitted us_mkt values: ν = {{ N.model.nu | f(2) }}, persistence
{{ N.model.persistence | f(4) }} (half-life {{ N.model.half_life | f(0) }} days), unconditional volatility
{{ N.model.uncond_vol | pct(1) }}.

### 3.2 Premium and its shrinkage

The sample mean excess return is {{ N.model.premium.raw_annual | pct(2) }} per year with a standard error of
{{ N.model.premium.se_annual | pct(2) }} ({{ N.model.premium.years | f(0) }} years). We shrink it
{{ N.model.premium.shrink_weight | pct(0) }} toward zero and use **{{ N.model.premium.annual_excess_used | pct(2) }}**.
The raw estimate is noisy, and the floor fails when the premium is overstated, not when it is understated. The same
procedure gives {{ N.fallback.premium_min | pct(2) }} to {{ N.fallback.premium_max | pct(2) }} across the
{{ N.deploy.n_assets }} assets. Because the premium is the input the DP leans on most, we calibrate the DP against a
halved premium and test it with a zero premium (§4.6, §6.3).

### 3.3 Rates, and why (σ_0, r_0) is the complete state

The short rate is Vasicek, fitted by OLS AR(1) on monthly rates from {{ N.model.rate.start }} to
{{ N.model.rate.end }} ({{ N.model.rate.n_months }} months). The 1954 start follows the Treasury–Fed Accord. Estimates:
κ = {{ N.model.rate.kappa | f(2) }} (SE {{ N.model.rate.se_kappa | f(2) }}), θ = {{ N.model.rate.theta | pct(2) }},
s = {{ N.model.rate.s | pct(2) }}, half-life {{ N.model.rate.half_life_years | f(1) }} years. The equity–rate
correlation is ρ = {{ N.model.rate.rho | f(2) }} (same-month pairing). Under GJR-GARCH the next day's return
distribution depends on the past only through σ_t², and the sign of the last shock is already in it. Under Vasicek the
rate's future depends only on r_t. So (σ_0, r_0) is everything the model can use at t = 0. The named states for us_mkt
are calm {{ ST.calm.sigma0 | pct(1) }}, normal {{ ST.normal.sigma0 | pct(1) }}, elevated
{{ ST.elevated.sigma0 | pct(1) }}, crisis {{ ST.crisis.sigma0 | pct(1) }} and extreme {{ ST.extreme.sigma0 | pct(1) }}
(the {{ N.data.extreme_day }} close), with r_0 = {{ ST.normal.r0 | pct(2) }} (the median). As checks: the model's σ_0
on 2020-03-16 is {{ N.model.anchors["2020-03-16"] | pct(1) }} and on 2008-11-20 {{ N.model.anchors["2008-11-20"] | pct(1) }}.

### 3.4 Validation

- **Stylised facts.** Simulated annual volatility is {{ N.model.stylised.sim_vol | pct(1) }} against
  {{ N.model.stylised.data_vol | pct(1) }} in the data. The 2% quantile of the 1-year cumulative excess log return is
  {{ N.model.stylised.q2_252_sim | pct(0) }} in the model and {{ N.model.stylised.q2_252_data | pct(0) }} in the data.
  At 2 years it is {{ N.model.stylised.q2_504_sim | pct(0) }} against {{ N.model.stylised.q2_504_data | pct(0) }}.
- **Conditional PIT test.** Each quarterly start is forecast with parameters estimated only on data before it
  (expanding window). We then count how often the realised 1- and 2-year return falls below the model's 2% quantile.
  Overall: {{ N.model.pit.H252.freq | pct(1) }} at 1 year (95% CI {{ N.model.pit.H252.ci_lo | pct(1) }}–{{ N.model.pit.H252.ci_hi | pct(1) }},
  effective n {{ N.model.pit.H252.n_eff | f(0) }}) and {{ N.model.pit.H504.freq | pct(1) }} at 2 years
  (CI {{ N.model.pit.H504.ci_lo | pct(1) }}–{{ N.model.pit.H504.ci_hi | pct(1) }}). No bucket or era rejects.
  **The test has low power:** per-bucket confidence intervals are {{ N.model.pit_bucket_ci_width_min | pp(0) }} to
  {{ N.model.pit_bucket_ci_width_max | pp(0) }} percentage points wide, and crisis starts are too few to test. The
  elevated-volatility point estimate at 1 year is {{ N.model.pit_elevated_252 | pct(1) }}. This is one reason we tune
  to a target below 2%. Full table: Appendix B.
- **Daily VaR backtest of the t tail** (`scripts/02b_var_backtest.py`). The fitted ν produces too many 1% and 0.1%
  exceedances, meaning the left tail is heavier than a symmetric t. That asymmetry is why filtered historical simulation,
  which resamples the empirical residuals, is in the robust set. ν = {{ S.fat_nu_min }} produces too few exceedances, meaning it is too
  heavy. So "fat tails (ν = {{ S.fat_nu_min }})" is a stress test, not part of the robust set.
- **Parameter stability.** Across sub-samples (1926–1962, from 1963, 1963–1999, from 1990, 2000 onward), persistence
  ranges from {{ N.model.subsample_persistence_min | f(3) }} to {{ N.model.subsample_persistence_max | f(3) }} and ν
  from {{ N.model.subsample_nu_min | f(1) }} to {{ N.model.subsample_nu_max | f(1) }} (Appendix B). The parameter
  uncertainty is carried into the robust set as a "parameter draws" scenario.

### 3.5 Known weaknesses

- **Thin unconditional long-horizon tail.** GJR's volatility mean-reverts in weeks, so it cannot generate 1929–32-style
  multi-year drawdowns (2-year 2% quantile: see §3.4). This matters less for a dynamic strategy than for a static one.
  Our strategies re-read σ_t daily and de-risk as a drawdown develops, so a slow crash is met with less equity. The
  danger is a fast crash, which is why gap risk drives the design (§4) and the stress tests (§6).
- **ind_telcm.** Its PIT rejects as too thin at 1 year ({{ N.deploy.flags.ind_telcm.pit_rejections }}). The window is
  dominated by the 2000–02 telecom bust. The deployment rule routes it to CPPI.
- **Automatic fallback window.** If the full-history fit is near-integrated (persistence ≥
  {{ S.fallback_max_persistence }}) or fewer than {{ S.fallback_min_draw_valid | pct(0) }} of parameter draws are
  valid, all equity estimation for that asset restarts at {{ S.fallback_start }}. It fired for
  {{ N.fallback.n_triggered }} assets ({{ N.fallback.assets | names }}). A **rule** beats hand-picked per-asset dates
  (we first used 1984 for telecom) because the committee will run the procedure on assets nobody has looked at. A
  hand-picked date also embeds hindsight about where the regime broke.

